{"agent_id":"pp3g-do7k-research-evidence-only-task-no-system-to-ship-no-5881","agent_seq":9,"timestamp":"2026-08-06T05:40:36.288418060Z","event":{"type":"IssueCreated","uuid":"b22a89ff-e016-4eab-9282-4e69d8ca729f","title":"VALIDATE (retry-classification): can tagging schema-validation failure as a terminal application error remove the 4x spawn multiplier (Test C of output-schema drift) — control vs classified-proxy before/after, plus real-connection-failure control","description":"Follow-on from #213 (output-schema drift). The drift test found a 4x spawn multiplier (4 backend spawns, 2 persistent proxies, per failed call) from TWO uncoordinated retry layers stacking: proxy-internal retry respawns the backend, then ToolRegistry's reconnect-retry (connection.py:106) treats the schema-validation failure as a connection problem and respawns the whole proxy. Test whether classification alone fixes it: the proxy returns a normal MCP tool-error response (clearly a schema/application failure, NOT a connection-close signature) and stops its own reflexive respawn for this failure class, so connection.py:106 has nothing to interpret as connection loss. Measure whether cost drops from ~4x to ~single-spawn. 3 tests: (1) reconfirm Test C 4x control; (2) modified proxy same drift scenario — spawn/proxy count, does reconnect-retry fire, latency vs control + #213 success baseline (1810-3124ms), error still clear; (3) real connection failure (kill proxy mid-call) still retries. Out of scope: building revalidation/fallback-fetch; re-testing bare-value diagnostics degradation. Evidence-only test scaffolding; do not modify ToolRegistry source.","priority":"medium","parent_uuid":"44a2f894-85dc-4396-a3c6-66dbc327ec3b","created_by":"pp3g-do7k-research-evidence-only-task-no-system-to-ship-no-5881"},"signed_by":"SHA256:PlJT+wlGJYlxgeZi2KczTX67l4yPIDGrZNHZ/YmcPEQ","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgVBbikeyA6X6HvbknLEFjjYqPuKnsdYRkhuwz6N/F35UAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAA